    "remark": "This submission extends our previously validated T9 artifact with a frozen T10 bit-axis BiGRU expert. A deterministic router selects experts using only the raw prime bit length. T10 uses a fixed, feedback-free raw-b two-pass schedule with raw-input-only width buckets; model predictions do not control routing, token order, masks, loop bounds, or schedule length. The T10 expert was warm-started from T9 and trained for 10,000 one-step AdamW updates (seed 56002).  \nFor transparency, this checkpoint remains below our stricter internal one-step promotion gate (999,990/1,000,000 versus 999,995 required), while prior fresh terminal T10 evaluation measured 97.53%. We preserve that research result separately rather than relabeling it.  \nWe thank participants in the SAIR Foundation Modular Arithmetic Challenge discussions for insights on recurrent arithmetic and fixed schedules. NeuralHorner by Robby Sneiderman and XllentAI/modular_arithmetic were useful methodological references; no code, weights, or generated artifacts were copied from them.",
